"Current discourse on LLM-MAS security pri- marily focuses on threats derived from agents, such as misinformation (Ju et al., 2024) and jailbreak attacks (Gu et al., 2024). The potential for these attacks to propagate within the system exacerbates the security challenges compared to single-agent contexts (Dong et al., 2025b; He et al., 2025). Recently, resource consumption attacks (RCAs) against LLMs (Gao et al., 2024; Dong et al., 2025a) have emerged as an urgent vulnerability similar to traditional Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks towards computer networks (Lau et al., 2000; Schuba et al., 1997). However, such RCAs fall short in capturing the distinct vulnerabilities from multi-agent collab- oration. Unlike DoS targeted network resources, or single-LLM attacks that induce repetitive gen- eration (Zhang et al., 2024b; Geiping et al., 2024), the threat we identify operates at the level of agent interaction. This attack turns the multi-agent col- laboration into a mechanism of systemic failure. To address this gap, we formalize a new threat class we term Denial-of-Collaboration (DoC). DoC attacks poison the communication of LLM-MASs, rendering each agent inactive, so the system re- mains functionally paralyzed, as illustrated in Fig. 1. As a practical instantiation, we further intro- duce the COntagious Recursive Blocking Attack (CORBA ), which operates by injecting instructions to achieve DoC. The instructions induce the agent6899 to perform a blocking action and propagate the complete instructions to all neighbors. By using the instructions following and topology (Yu et al., 2024), CORBA forces all agents into the infinite loop, weaponizing the system\u2019s communications. Our experiments show that CORBA achieves an attack success rate of 85% on average on two pop- ular open-source LLM-MAS frameworks, Auto- Gen (Wu et al., 2024) and Camel (Li et al., 2023). In contrast, conventional prompt injection fail to propagate systemically, a result that highlights the novelty and potency of our contagious nature."

